.TH SEARCH 1
.SH NAME
Search, srch \- find anything by part of it, in acme
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Search
[
.B -s
.I letters
] [
.I dir
|
.I query ...
]
.PP
.B srch/files
.I root
.br
.B srch/grep
.I root pattern
.br
.B srch/windows
.I root
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Search
opens an
.IR acme (1)
window,
.IB dir /+Search\f1.
Its first line is a query, and the lines after it are what matches it, best first, as many as a
hundred and at most five from one file, changing as the query is typed.
A result is a file, or a file and a line,
.IB file : line
followed by the line's text.
.PP
The results come from sources, programs
.BI /bin/srch/ name
that
.B /lib/search
names, or
.B $home/lib/search
in its place: one a line, a letter, a name, and
.B static
or
.BR query .
A static source runs once, as
.BI srch/ name " root"\f1,
and its lines are filtered as the query is typed;
a query source runs again, as
.BI srch/ name " root pattern"\f1,
at each pause in typing, stopping the run before it.
The sources are
.B w
(acme's windows),
.B f
(files) and
.B g
(the lines of files, by
.IR grep (1)).
.B -s
names the sources searched; with none, all.
A query ending
.BI + letters
searches those alone.
.PP
A query is words separated by blanks, and a result must match each:
a word's letters in order, counting for more at the start of a name or a word, in a run, and in
a file's own name;
.BI ' word
the word itself;
.BI ^ word
at the start;
.IB word $
at the end; and
.BI ! word
not anywhere.
A word with no upper-case letter ignores case.
A query source is given the words to look for, without their marks, as its pattern.
.PP
A newline typed at the end of the query opens the first result;
button 3 or button 2 on a result opens that one, by the plumber's
.B edit
port, at its line.
The window stays, with its query, until
.BR Del .
Given text that is not a directory, as when
.I Search
is run with a chorded argument,
.I Search
starts with that text as its query.
.PP
The files are those of
.I dir
and below, as
.IR walk (1)
gives them, without
.BR .git ;
or, in a git repository with the
.B git9
package added (see
.IR pkg (1)),
its tracked files, by
.BR git/walk ;
and
.I dir
is the repository's top.
With no
.IR dir ,
.I Search
takes the directory of the file in acme window
.B $acmewin
or
.BR $winid ,
or else the current directory.
.PP
When a rule of
.B $home/lib/lsp
or
.B /lib/lsp
(see
.IR lspfs (4))
maps a directory above
.I dir
to another machine,
.B map="\fIhere there\fP"
with
.B cmd="ssh \fIhost\fP ..."\f1,
the sources run there:
.I Search
gives them
.B $host
and
.BR $hostroot ,
.IR dir 's
place on
.IR host .
.B srch/files
and
.B srch/grep
then list and search the files by
.IR ssh (1)
on
.I host
rather than through the mount, and the paths they print are under
.IR dir .
.SH EXAMPLES
.I Acmestart
binds Command-P and Command-Shift-F in
.BR /lib/hotkey/acme :
.IP
.EX
cmd-p	{test -e /mnt/acme/index || mount $acmesrv /mnt/acme; Search -s wf}
cmd-F	{test -e /mnt/acme/index || mount $acmesrv /mnt/acme; Search -s g}
.EE
.PP
Find a function's callers by grep alone, but not its tests:
.IP
.EX
decodeToken !_test+g
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/search
.TP
.B /lib/search
the sources
.TP
.B /bin/srch
their programs
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/Search.c
and
.B rc/bin/srch
.SH SEE ALSO
